"Intersection sizes of linear subspaces with the hypercube (https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.02729)\nciteturn6academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.1 years ago; Authors: Carla Groenland, Tom Johnston ... We continue the study by Melo and Winter [arXiv:1712.01763, 2017] on the possible intersection sizes of a $k$-dimensional subspace with the vertices of the $n$-dimensional hypercube in Euclidean space.\nTitle: Intersection sizes of linear subspaces with the hypercube\nAuthors: Carla Groenland, Tom Johnston\nDate: Fri Oct  5 14:52:23 2018\n\nWe continue the study by Melo and Winter [arXiv:1712.01763, 2017] on the possible intersection sizes of a $k$-dimensional subspace with the vertices of the $n$-dimensional hypercube in Euclidean space. Melo and Winter conjectured that all intersection sizes larger than $2^{k-1}$ (the \"large\" sizes) are of the form $2^{k-1}+2^i$. We show that this is almost true: the large intersection sizes are either of this form or of the form $35\\cdot 2^{k-6}$. We also disprove a second conjecture of Melo and Winter by proving that a positive fraction of the \"small\" values is missing.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntersection sizes of linear subspaces with the hypercube (https://tomjohnston.co.uk/papers/intersection-sizes-of-linear-subspaces-with-the-hypercube.pdf)\nciteturn6search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.9 years ago; Carla Groenland and Tom Johnston* ... We continue the study by Melo and Winter [arXiv:1712.01763, 2017] on the possible intersection sizes of a *k*-dimensional subspace with the vertices of the *n*-dimensional hypercube in Euclidean space.\nIntersection sizes of linear subspaces with the hypercube\n\nCarla Groenland and Tom Johnston*\n\nDecember 27, 2018\n\n**Abstract**\n\nWe continue the study by Melo and Winter [arXiv:1712.01763, 2017] on the possible intersection sizes of a *k*-dimensional subspace with the vertices of the *n*-dimensional hypercube in Euclidean space. Melo and Winter conjectured that all intersection sizes larger than 2<sup>k−1</sup> (the “large” sizes) are of the form 2<sup>k−1</sup> + 2<sup>i</sup>. We show that this is almost true: the large intersection sizes are either of this form or of the form 35·2<sup>k−6</sup>. We also disprove a second conjecture of Melo and Winter by proving that a positive fraction of the “small” values is missing.\n\n# 1 Introduction\n\nWhat possible intersection sizes can the hypercube H<sup>n</sup> = {0, 1}<sup>n</sup> have with a *k*-dimensional linear subspace *S* of ℝ<sup>n</sup>? The smallest possible intersection size is 1 and the largest 2<sup>k</sup>, but which numbers in between are possible? Melo and Winter [3] initiated the study of the sets H(*k*, *m*) of the possible intersection sizes of a *k*-dimensional subspace with the (*m* + *k*)-dimensional hypercube. Melo and Winter made two conjectures about the structure of H(*k*, ∞) = ⋃<sub>m∈ℕ</sub> H(*k*, *m*), depending on whether the intersection size is “large” (greater than 2<sup>k−1</sup>) or “small” (smaller than 2<sup>k−1</sup>). Let H<sup>+</sup>(*k*, *m*) be the large values of H(*k*, *m*), i.e.\n\nH<sup>+</sup>(*k*, *m*) = H(*k*, *m*) \\ {1, . . . , 2<sup>k−1</sup>}\n\n*Mathematical Institute, University of Oxford, Oxford, OX2 6GG, United Kingdom.  \nE-mail: {carla.groenland,thomas.johnston}@maths.ox.ac.uk.*\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntersection sizes of linear subspaces with the hypercube - TU Delft Research Portal (https://research.tudelft.nl/en/publications/intersection-sizes-of-linear-subspaces-with-the-hypercube/)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Dive into the research topics of 'Intersection sizes of linear subspaces with the hypercube'. ... Groenland, C & Johnston, T 2020, 'Intersection sizes of linear subspaces with the hypercube', Journal of Combinatorial Theory.\n\n# Intersection sizes of linear subspaces with the hypercube\n\n  * Carla Groenland\n  * , Tom Johnston\n\nResearch output: Contribution to journal › Article › Scientific › peer-review\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe continue the study by Melo and Winter (2019) [3] on the possible intersection sizes of a k-dimensional subspace with the vertices of the n-dimensional hypercube in Euclidean space. Melo and Winter conjectured that all intersection sizes larger than 2^{k−1} (the “large” sizes) are of the form 2^{k−1}+2^{i}. We show that this is almost true: the large intersection sizes are either of this form or of the form 35⋅2^{k−6}. We also disprove a second conjecture of Melo and Winter by proving that a positive fraction of the “small” values is missing.\n\nOriginal language  | English\nArticle number  | 105142\nJournal  | Journal of Combinatorial Theory. Series A\nVolume  | 170\nDOIs  | \n\n  * https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcta.2019.105142\nPublication status  | Published - 2020\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChapter 7\n\nIntersection sizes of linear subspaces (https://cgroenland.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/thesiswithcover.pdf)\nciteturn6search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Crawled: 5.2 years ago; Melo and Winter [94] initiated the study of the sets \\(H(k,m)\\) of possible intersection sizes that a \\(k\\)-dimensional linear subspace can have with the \\((k+m)\\)-dimensional hypercube. ... This chapter is based on joint work with Tom Johnston [52].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn cordial hypertrees (https://www.iwogl2016.agh.edu.pl/abstracts/Tuczynski.pdf)\nciteturn6search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; hypergraphs without cycles) are k-cordial for all k. We prove the conjecture ... theorem [3] which states that every tree is k-cordial for k = 3, 4.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn cordial labeling of 